  "preamble": "Stopping of the voiced dental fricative [ð] to [d]. It happened historically in West Germanic (fricative [ð] > [d] in all positions), in North Germanic Swedish (Old Norse góðr > god), and in urban Egyptian and Levantine Arabic (ذ > d). It is also a well-known feature of many English dialects (th-stopping). Within Semitic, Aramaic (Syriac diqnā 'beard'), Soqotri (dínob 'tail') and Maltese (denb) also stopped *ð to d, and Ugaritic texts show the merger under way.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Stopping, Fortition",
